+*The floor.*  With the tactic `conflict_floor`, off by default, a check keeps `K` unless `K` weighs at least 3
+(`CONFLICT_FLOOR`), that is unless `K` has three or more members that cost the bound almost nothing to leave out.  The
+look finds that a conflict with one such member can be the better one to branch on, though the weight rates it worse;
+the floor extends that to two.  On the units of the hardest games of the chain the search's own conflict at the top
+often has two members of price 8, and there the search's own conflict closed the unit in a fraction of the steps that
+the conflict chosen took.  The floor is exact for the reason the choice is: it only chooses between `K` and the
+conflict chosen, both true conflicts.  No checkpoint records it, so the run that resumes a search says whether it
+applies.
+
+*What the floor does.*  At the gate of 1,200, on the units of games 4983 and 4984 that finished, it takes 0.87 and
+0.90 of the CPU without it, and on those of games 4991, 4992 and 4995 1.04 to 1.18, so it is off by default and was
+not adopted.  The measurements are in [a node-by-node rule for conflict
+branching](../../notebook/search/a-node-by-node-rule-for-conflict-branching-2026-10/README.md).
+
